<% content_for :title, "Every document you own has a deadline" %>

<h1>Every document you own has a deadline.<br>We tell you which one is next.</h1>

<p class="lede">
  A calendar reminder says "passport expires 14 March". It does not say "start in November, because
  renewing from abroad takes ten weeks and needs a police certificate you have not ordered yet."
  The failure is not memory. It is lead time.
</p>

<section class="answer">
  <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
  <p class="headline"><a href="<%= playbooks_path %>">Browse the renewal playbooks</a></p>
  <p>
    <%= pluralize(@playbook_count, "playbook") %> across <%= pluralize(@rules.size, "document") %>
    and <%= pluralize(@country_count, "issuing country") %>, each computed from an open dataset of
    renewal rules rather than guessed.
  </p>
</section>

<h2>Why this is different</h2>

<div class="cards">
  <div class="card">
    <h3>It knows the process, not just the date</h3>
    <p class="note">
      Renewal windows, processing times, and the prerequisites that have to be obtained first —
      so the answer is a date to start, not a date to panic.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>It handles more than one country at once</h3>
    <p class="note">
      Renewing from Doha is not renewing from London: different queue, different mission, different
      paperwork. Every playbook is written per origin.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>It admits what it does not know</h3>
    <p class="note">
      Every figure carries a last-verified date and a link to the authority. Unverified rules say so
      on the page, loudly.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>It is open</h3>
    <p class="note">
      The rules are a public dataset. Anyone can correct one, and nobody has to trust us about a
      number they can check themselves.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
